Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This product includes software developed by third parties. The complete
inventory ships in THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md. That file is generated from the
actual release artifacts, not maintained by hand:

    pwsh -File packaging/demo-v1/collect-licenses.ps1 `
      -LibDir release/edb-demo-v1/cli/lib `
      -ArchivePath release/edb-demo-v1/server/EXPERIMENT_DATA_BRIDGE.jar `
      -OutputPath THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md

Of the entries whose licence metadata resolves automatically, the majority are
Apache-2.0; MIT, ISC, BSD-3-Clause and EDL-1.0 are also present.

Obligations to honour before publishing a binary distribution
-------------------------------------------------------------

1. MySQL Connector/J 9.4.0 - GPLv2 with the Universal FOSS Exception.
   Verified from the artifact itself: the licensing manual bundled at the root
   of the fat JAR contains both the "Universal FOSS Exception" and the GPLv2
   text. The FOSS Exception is what permits this combination, and it applies
   because EDB is distributed as open source under Apache-2.0. If EDB were ever
   relicensed as proprietary, MySQL Connector/J would have to be replaced - for
   example with MariaDB Connector/J (LGPL-2.1).

2. org.eclipse.jgit 7.2.1.202505142326-r - Eclipse Distribution License v1.0,
   a BSD-3-Clause variant. This is stated in the about.html bundled inside the
   artifact; it is not licensed under the EPL.

3. Test-scope dependencies (JUnit 5, H2, mockk, Testcontainers) are not part of
   the distributed runtime and are not redistributed in the release package.

4. THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.md lists entries as UNKNOWN when an artifact ships no
   machine-readable licence metadata. This is common in the Kotlin/JetBrains
   ecosystem. Those entries must be resolved by hand before binaries are
   published; the list is a work item, not an assertion that they are
   unlicensed.

